\section*{Weak Hessian bounds along every RCD geodesic}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Weak Hessians on every geodesic}: The manuscript claims that an upper distributional Hessian bound for a bounded Lipschitz function on a finite-dimensional RCD space gives the corresponding second-derivative bound along every minimizing geodesic. The upper-bound function is required to be bounded and continuous.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} On a full-support unreduced $\operatorname{RCD}(K,N)$ space, $K\in\mathbb R$, $1<N<\infty$, let $F$ be bounded and globally Lipschitz, and $G$ bounded and continuous. For compactly supported test $g\in D(\Delta)$, bounded and globally Lipschitz with $\Delta g\in W^{1,2}$, put $Y=\nabla g$ and \[H_F(Y,Y)(h)=-\int\operatorname{div}(hY)\langle\nabla F,Y\rangle\,dm-\int h\langle\nabla F,\nabla_YY\rangle\,dm.\] Suppose $H_F(\nabla g,\nabla g)(h)\le\int hG|\nabla g|^2\,dm$ for every such $g$ and nonnegative compactly supported Lipschitz $h$. Then, for any minimizing geodesic $\sigma:[0,1]\to M$ parameterized at constant speed $\ell$, the claim says it is claimed to satisfy \[(F\circ\sigma)^{\prime\prime}\le\ell^2G\circ\sigma\] in distributions on $(0,1)$. The conclusion is for every geodesic, not only almost every geodesic of a test plan.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Weak Hessians on every geodesic}: The measure is multiplied by an exponential of the function, giving a weighted Bochner inequality with shifted curvature. Resolvent regularity and a common graph core place this inequality on the actual weighted generator domain. Curvature-dimension comparison for the weighted measures and a large-parameter limit extract the desired inequality on any prescribed geodesic.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Weak Hessians on every geodesic}: The manuscript claims that an upper distributional Hessian bound for a bounded Lipschitz function on a finite-dimensional RCD space gives the corresponding second-derivative bound along every minimizing geodesic. The upper-bound function is required to be bounded and continuous. The parameters, regularity assumptions and normalization in this question are retained in applying that assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 356 describes Lean coverage for the weak-Hessian inequality on every geodesic; the full sectional-curvature characterization is separate.

\textit{Weak Hessians on every geodesic}: The manuscript links \texttt{WeakHessian.lean} (declaration \texttt{OAI.WeakHessian.every\_geodesic}).

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

